d what  she's done  to deserve that but yes any moment that  beth is  alone i think tells you a great deal  about her and it's also telling that  that's  a big part of when she does partake in  substances learning about addiction  one of the things i found most  interesting was that whatever it is that  the addict  is getting from the substance clearly  worked for a period of time  the awful thing about addiction is that  it is guaranteed to stop working and it  will completely derail your life and it  was very important for both scott and i  that you understood as the audience oh  this is beth drinking because she's  lonely this is beth taking the pills  because she feels too much  this is her drinking as a form of  punishment this is her drinking as a  form of celebration and like  understanding exactly what it is that  she's getting from it  um each time there is one player that  scares me  oh the russian  the chess sequences and the substance  addiction sequences were very  important to us and it was something  that scott and i spoke a lot about  at the very very beginning on our first  meeting because you know the show is  long it is seven hours and there's a lot  of chess and there's a lot of substance  abuse and so we had to make sure that  each time  it was fresh and exciting and it didn't  just feel like we were recycling  something consistently  and the beauty of chess is that it is  played usually between two people so  whoever is  opposite beth in whichever situation  does elicit a different reaction from  her  two of my favorite chess sequences are  the first time that beth plays towns  because it's the first time that she  doesn't necessarily want to beat someone  like it hurts her to win in the brutal  way that she does because  you know she likes a boy for the first  time  and the other one that i adore is the  first time beth meets a prodigy who is  younger than her  that gets her that really gets to her  that like  undermines her entire belief of self and  she just  i i asked scott i was like do i have  permission to like  fully attempt to psych out this kid like  i want to be  up i want to be moving i want to be  circling around in like a shark and she  was like  go for it and i didn't tell um i didn't  tell the boy that i was  playing with i was gonna do that and uh  it felt pretty good  tell the readers of life how it feels  and to be a girl  among all those men i don't mind it  one of the things that i find most  fascinating about beth  and something i think will resonate with  people is the fact that because she is  so intelligent because she sort of  exists in this kind of  like just outside of society even though  this is set in the 60s  she is genuinely baffled that people  care about her gender  like genuinely does not understand why  people are talking about her being a  girl  rather than the thrashing that she just  you know delivered to  whoever it was that she was playing and  having that  incredulousness having that lack of  understanding of like wait why does this  matter  was a wonderful thing to be able to  portray especially in the 60s but i  think something that will resonate with  a lot of people now today  because it's almost like she wasn't  gifted  the handbook that says you're a girl  this is what you're allowed to want  this is what you're allowed to dream  about this is what you're allowed to say  this is what you're allowed to  embody and because of that it very  quickly becomes that she's not  out there to be the best female chess  player she's out there to be the best  chess player period  and i hope that as a society we get to  move towards  a world where regardless of your gender  how you identify you're allowed to want  whatever you want and you're allowed to  follow whatever dream that you want to  follow  chess isn't always competitive  jess can also be  beautiful the fashion of queen's gambit  was so much fun  um i feel very very lucky that  scott felt like he had cast the right  person and so  i had a big heart to play within like  how we were going to  dress her up like the way that she was  going to look um the first thing i said  to scott was she needs to have red hair  like that was just something imperative  to me um because i wanted her to stick  out like a sore thumb wherever she went  and the journey that we go through with  beth in the clothes i think is  fascinating on two levels the first  being that  the age that she is when i get to start  playing her  is the age where typically people are  trying on different personas like  trying to see where they fit and i had  this idea that she sort of  would watch movies or like have a muse  and try and emulate that so you know she  goes to school and she sees the girls  there for the first time so she starts  to try and dress like them  and realizes ah now that doesn't work  for me then she sees her first audrey  hepburn movie and all of a sudden it's  you know the high neck black t-shirts  and um  cigarette pants and and it was just such  a great way of charging where she was  going and one of the conversations that  we had very early on was  people worrying that she was too  glamorous and i just thought that that  was the whole point  that it wasn't mutually exclusive that  you can be a brainiac  and like nice clothes at the same time  like it's not such a crazy expression  anymore  um and i love that she is  unapologetically feminine in that  because i think it would have been very  easy to go  oh she's a chess like genius so that  means that she obviously doesn't care  about clothes and i'm like  nah that's not the way that the story's  gonna go you just let them blow by  and you go on ahead and do just what how  you feel like  i think potentially some of the  experiences that i've had  um as myself really helped prepare me to  play beth i think  i was so lucky and i i genuinely  mean this i was so lucky that the first  people that i worked with  never made me feel like because i was  young or a girl i did not have a seat at  the table  and so when i showed up on the witch i  was a collaborator with robert you know  it was never like you hush up you sit  there you do what i tell you to do it  was a collaboration and i think  much in the same way that beth  experiences it in the chess world i was  sort of really shocked the first time  that i ever experienced like true sexism  on set i was like oh wow i've been  spared that  for ages um  so i think i had i had some of my own  uh experiences to draw to draw from  uh but by the time beth was done i i was  i was ready it was it was good  you
 

